<?php

// Legacy help.lib.php bcoem_dashboard_help() bodies for the nine
// dashboard sections. Internal legacy links were reduced to their text.

return [
    'comp-prep' => ['title' => 'Competition Preparation Help', 'body' => '<p>The Competition Preparation category houses links to areas of your site that are intended to be completed before account or entry registration opens. Much of this information was completed during the set up process.</p><p>Expand the category by selecting the heading or icon. Once expanded, you are presented with several choices. You can:</p><ol><li>Edit all entry-related, account-related, judging and non-judging session, results publication, and awards-related dates associated with the competition.<li>Edit competition info including BJCP Competition ID (if applicable), registraion, entry, and drop-off windows, shipping location address, rules, awards, and other information.</li><li>Manage or add competition contacts.<li>Manage or add custom categories &ndash; useful if the competition features unique “best of show” categories, such as Pro-Am opportunities, Stewards’ Choice, Best Name, etc.<li>Manage or add entry drop-off locations and associated information.<li>Manage or add judging locations and associated information.<li>Manage or add sponsors.</li><li>Manage or add accepted styles &ndash; these can also include any competition-specific styles.</li><li>Manage or add style types (e.g., beer, mead, cider, etc.). BCOE&M ships with eight pre-defined style types.</li><li>Upload a competition logo or sponsor logos.</li></ol>'],
    'entries-participants' => ['title' => 'Entries and Participants Help', 'body' => '<p>Once registration has opened for your competition, you can keep track of ongoing participant and entry data by using the functions under the Entries and Participants heading.<p>Expand the category by selecting the heading or icon. Once expanded, you are presented with several choices. You can:</p><ol><li>Manage entries.<li>Manage participants.<li>Assign judges, stewards, or staff to their respective pools.</li><li>Register a participant.</li><li>Register a judge or steward, inputting all registration data.</li><li>Quick Register a judge or steward, inputting minimal registration data. This option is optimal for adding judges or stewards to the pool on-the-fly (usually on the day of the judging).</li></ol>'],
    'sorting' => ['title' => 'Entry Sorting Help', 'body' => '<p>Once the registration period and entry window for your competition passes, the next step in the process, after picking up entries from various drop off points and the designated shipping location, is to sort them. BCOE&amp;M provides tools to assist in sorting entries.</p><p>Under the Entry Sorting heading, administrators are presented with some options specific to the task, namely:<ul><li>Regenerating/replacing the system-assigned judging numbers. If your competition is not utilizing the barcode check-in feature, administrators can make extra sure that all entries in the database are assigned a unique judging number by activating this function. <strong>This should be done after the entry window has closed.</strong></li><li>Checking-in entries (marking them as paid and received) manually or, if enabled, via barcode scanner.</li><li>Printing sorting sheets by entry numbers or judging numbers.</li><li>Printing entry number/judging number cheat sheets organized by style.</li><li>Printing labels to attach to individual bottles with their unique entry or judging number.</li><li>Reports to help to sort entries into their associated tables (this requires the use of Tables Planning Mode &ndash; see the Organizing section to enable).</li></ul>'],
    'organizing' => ['title' => 'Organizing Help', 'body' => "<p>These functions help Admins to organize the competition data in preparation for judging. Organization is really where the <em>M</em> in BCOE&amp;M shows its utility.</p><p>Organization in BCOE&amp;M begins with assigning individual participants as a staff member and/or judge or steward. This builds a pool of available participants to assign to various duties in the competition.</p><p>Once assignments have been given, the next steps are to:</p><ol><li>Define tables where specific sub-styles will be judged.</li><li>Add flights to tables (if queued judging is disabled).</li><li>Assign tables to rounds.</li><li>Assign judges and stewards to tables (and flights, if applicable).</li></ol><div class=\"alert alert-info\"><h5 style=\'font-size: 1.1em;\'>Tables Planning Mode vs. Tables Competition Mode</h5><p>When the <strong>Tables Planning Mode</strong> function is enabled, Admins can define tables, flights, rounds, judge/steward assignments, and associated entry limits (if enabled) <em>prior</em> to entries being marked as paid and/or received (i.e., prior to sorting). Any table configurations and associated assignments <em>will not be official</em> until an Admin returns to Tables Competition Mode after entries have been sorted and marked as received in the system. <em>Pullsheets will <u>not</u> be available</em>.</p><p>When the <strong>Tables Competition Mode</strong> function is enabled by an Admin, it indicates to the system that the planning stage is over and <em>all applicable entries have been marked as <u>received</u></em>. Table configurations and assignments can still be changed as necessary while in Competition Mode. Pullsheets will be available.</p></div><p>Once these tasks have been completed, you are ready to print reports you need prior to judging: pullsheets, table cards, assignment lists, sign-in sheets, scoresheet lables, and name tags. See the Reports section.</p>"],
    'scoring' => ['title' => 'Scoring Help', 'body' => '<p>During or after the initial round of judging, competition organizers manage the assigned score and place, if applicable, for each entry.</p><p>Scores can be recorded in one of two ways, depending upon whether Electronic Scoresheets are enabled and utilized by judges to record their evaluations of each entry.</p><ol><li>If Electronic Scoresheets are enabled, individual and consensus scores are recorded by judges with their evaluations. However, please note that <strong>scores and associated places are not official until an Admin imports the matching consensus scores into the scores database</strong>. This can be done via the Administration: Entry Evaluations function.<li>If not using Electronic Scoresheets, competition organizers can manually enter the assigned score and place, if applicable, for each entry.</li></ol><p>From this pool of scores, BCOE&amp;M then determines which entry or entries move on to the Best of Show round by style type (see the Administration: Style Types function to determine the BOS Pull Method for each).<p><p class="well"><small>For example, administrators can designate that only the 1st place entries for the Beer style type may move to the BOS round, but the 1st, 2nd, and 3rd place from the Mead and Cider categories move on.</small></p><p>Once all scores and places have been recorded for the initial round of judging, Administrators can then view and print pullsheets for the Best of Show round.</p><p>After the BOS round has completed, administrators can then enter the BOS winners for each style type in addition to any applicable custom category winners.</p>'],
    'preferences' => ['title' => 'Preferences Help', 'body' => '<p>BCOE&amp;M offers several ways to customize the behavior of your BCOE&amp;M installation. Preferences are divided into six salient categories:</p><ol><li>General Preferences include the competition type (amateur or professional), admin theme, results date and time display, winner distribution method (by medal group, by style, or by sub-style), CAPTCHA, and localization.</p><li>Entry Preferences include style set, entry label options, entry fees and discounts, and any overall entry limits, per-style type and per-style limits.</p><li>Email Sending / Contact Display Preferences are those that govern whether or not the BCOE&amp;M system can send emails. To enable the system to send emails, you will need to provide a valid email address and associated information to send emails via the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP).</p><li>Currency and Payment Preferences allow for defining of accepted currency and the methods the competition will employ to collect fees (cash, check, PayPal).</p><li>Best Brewer and/or Best Club Preferences allows admins to define and display a list of best brewers and/or clubs according to defined points and tie break rules. Best Brewers and Best Clubs will be displayed with overall winners.</p><li>Judging/Competition Organization Preferences are those affect how BCOE&amp;M behaves with regard to tables, flights, and best of show functions.</p><ol>'],
    'reports' => ['title' => 'Reports Help', 'body' => '<p>BCOE&amp;M offers several options for printing and reporting competition related data and results. In general, the reports generated fall into three categories: before judging, during judging, and after judging.</p><p><strong>Before Judging</strong></p><ul><li>Pullsheets for each table defined in the system, using entry numbers or judging numbers.</li><li>Table cards for each table.</li><li>Assignment lists for judges and stewards.</li><li>Sign-in sheets for judges and stewards.</li><li>Judge, steward, and staff name tags.</li><li>Judge scoresheet labels in letter (<a class="hide-loader" href="http://www.avery.com/avery/en_us/Products/Labels/Addressing-Labels/Easy-Peel-White-Address-Labels_05160.htm" target="_blank">Avery 5160) and A4 (<a class="hide-loader" href="http://www.avery.se/avery/en_se/Products/Labels/Multipurpose/White-Multipurpose-Labels-Permanent/General-Usage-Labels-White_3422.htm" target="_blank">Avery 3422) format.</li></ul><p><strong>During Judging</strong></p><ul><li>Best of Show (BOS), Mini-BOS, and Pro-Am/Scale-Up pullsheets. These are inteneded to be printed in landscape format.</li><li>Best of Show (BOS), Mini-BOS, and Pro-Am/Scale-Up cup mats for judges to use while judging the their respective rounds. These are inteneded to be printed in landscape format.</li></ul><p><strong>After Judging</strong></p><ul><li>Results reports, with and without scores - print, PDF, or HTML.</li><li>A Best of Show (BOS) results report - print, PDF, or HTML.</li><li>A Best Brewer / Best Club results report (if applicable) - print only.</li><li>A BJCP points report in three formats - print, PDF, or XML.</li><li>Participant address labels for mailing scoresheets, medals, and/or prizes.</li><li>Participant summaries - cover sheets to include with mailed scoresheets or medals.</li><li>A post-judging inventory detailing possible leftover bottles and their corresponding entry number, judging number, and style.</li></ul>'],
    'data-exports' => ['title' => 'Data Exports Help', 'body' => '<p>Under the Data Exports heading, BCOE&amp;M features exporting options intended to provide administrators competition related data in CSV format &ndash; easily readable by spreadsheet programs such as Microsoft Excel, Google Sheets, or Open Office Calc.</p><p>Available exports include:</p><ul><li>Participant-related data, including contact information for judges, stewards, staff, and any winners (for awards or circuit reporting).</li><li>Entry-related data, including dumps of all associated data, paid, received, and non-paid entries, and entries associated with participants.</li></ul>'],
    'data-mgmt' => ['title' => 'Data Management Help', 'body' => '<p>Under the Data Management heading, top-level administrators can periodically make sure that data integrity is correct for their installation.</p><p>Included is the ability to manually trigger BCOE&amp;M&rsquo;s Data Clean-up function that checks for any duplicate or empty rows in the user/participant, entry, and all associated judging tables.</p><p>Also included is the ability to purge various data, including entries, non-Admin participants, judging tables, scores, custom styles, judge/steward/staff availability, and uploaded scoresheets.</p><p>For non-hosted installations, an archive option is available.</p>'],
];
